    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d892">

                <pb facs="0993" n="983" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg983"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1161/8–1044: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-10T09:00:00+03:00">August 10, 1944—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 10—8:54 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2913. Personal for the Acting Secretary. ReDepts 1885, August 8, 8 p.m.<anchor
                        n="92" xml:id="d892-f1"/> I need guidance on how much pressure I am to exert
                    in regard to visa for General Fleming. I have written Molotov a letter, spoken
                    in detail with Vyshinski who seemed well disposed to the visit, and followed up
                    this talk with a further letter, in each case asking for a prompt reply. My
                    experience is that if I bring maximum pressure on the Foreign Office by calling
                    them up daily and finally to the point of rudeness insisting on action, I have
                    been successful in getting visas through. I have followed this procedure in
                    connection with military personnel or others vital to the prosecution of the war
                    and of immediate importance. It is my opinion that I can only do this sort of
                    thing without causing offense when the visitors are of real importance to our
                    common interests.</p>
                <p>In General Fleming’s case I suggest that I write a courteous letter to Molotov or
                    Vyshinski stating that unless Fleming’s visa is approved by whatever date may be
                    specified, it will be too late for him to make the trip. I believe this will get
                    favorable action but cannot be sure. I request instructions.<anchor n="93"
                        xml:id="d892-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d892-f1" n="92" xml:id="d892-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested the Ambassador to try to hasten the issuance of a visa
                    for General Fleming (033.1161/7–1544).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d892-f2" n="93" xml:id="d892-f2-content">
                <p>The Ambassador’s proposal was agreed to by the Department in telegram 1927,
                    August 12, 1944 (033.1161/8–1244).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
